/* data/detail-c/phy-12-03.js - GENERATED by tools/build.py from data/detail/phy-12-03.json (our own words). Do not edit. */
(window.DETAIL_C = window.DETAIL_C || {})["phy-12-03"] = {"phy-12-03-m01":{"idea":"The ideal gas equation links pressure, volume, amount of gas and temperature in one formula.","steps":["The three gas laws are Boyle's, Charles's and Avogadro's.","Together they give P V proportional to n T.","Adding the constant R (the universal gas constant) gives PV = nRT.","Here n is the number of moles and T is the absolute temperature in kelvin."],"others":"PV = nR/T puts T in the wrong place: pressure would fall as T rises. P/V = nRT fails because P and V are multiplied in the law. PT = nRV is not the law either, and its units do not match.","trap":"Check units: PV is energy (J), and nRT is mol × J/(mol K) × K = J.","answer":"PV = nRT"},"phy-12-03-m02":{"idea":"Gas pressure comes from molecules hitting the walls. Kinetic theory turns this into a formula using density and speed.","steps":["Molecules move randomly, so on average only one third of the motion is along any one direction.","Each wall hit gives a push; the total push per area is the pressure.","This gives P = ⅓ ρ v_rms².","Here ρ is the density and v_rms is the root-mean-square speed."],"others":"½ρv_rms² looks like kinetic energy per volume, but the factor is ⅓, not ½. ⅓ρv_rms has speed to the first power, so its units are wrong. ρv_rms² misses the ⅓ from the three directions.","trap":"Quick check: ρv² has units of pressure (kg/m³ × m²/s²). The ⅓ is the only free part.","answer":"⅓ρv_rms²"},"phy-12-03-m03":{"idea":"Temperature measures the average translational kinetic energy of one molecule.","steps":["Each molecule can move in three independent directions: x, y and z.","Every degree of freedom carries an average energy of ½kT.","So the total translational energy is 3 × ½kT = (3/2)kT.","Here k is Boltzmann's constant and T is the absolute temperature."],"others":"(1/2)kT is the energy of just one degree of freedom, not all three. 3kT is double the real value. (3/2)RT is correct for one mole, not for one molecule, because R = N_A k.","trap":"k goes with one molecule; R goes with one mole.","answer":"(3/2)kT"},"phy-12-03-m04":{"idea":"The rms speed of molecules depends on the square root of the absolute temperature.","steps":["The formula is v_rms = √(3kT/m), so v_rms is proportional to √T.","The new temperature is 4T.","New speed ∝ √(4T) = 2 √T.","So the speed becomes twice as large."],"others":"Four times as large forgets the square root. Sixteen times as large squares the factor instead of taking its root. Half as large would need the temperature to fall, not rise.","trap":"Speed goes as √T; kinetic energy goes as T.","answer":"twice as large"},"phy-12-03-m05":{"idea":"At the same temperature, lighter molecules move faster. The rms speed goes as 1/√M.","steps":["v_rms = √(3RT/M), so at equal T the speed is proportional to 1/√M.","M is the molar mass: 2 for hydrogen and 32 for oxygen.","v_H / v_O = √(M_O / M_H) = √(32/2) = √16 = 4.","So the ratio is 4 : 1."],"others":"2 : 1 would be the answer if speed went as 1/M. 1 : 4 turns the ratio upside down; hydrogen is the faster gas. 16 : 1 forgets the square root.","trap":"The heavier gas is slower. Take the square root of the mass ratio, not the ratio itself.","answer":"4 : 1"},"phy-12-03-m06":{"idea":"Degrees of freedom count the independent ways a molecule can store energy.","steps":["A monatomic molecule is a point. It can only move along x, y and z, so f = 3.","A rigid diatomic molecule also has translation in 3 directions.","It can rotate about 2 axes perpendicular to the bond. Rotation about the bond line itself has no effect.","So f = 3 + 2 = 5 for diatomic."],"others":"7 for diatomic would include vibration, which is not active at moderate temperature. 3 for diatomic misses the two rotations. 6 for diatomic would need a third rotation, which a line-like molecule does not have.","trap":"Vibration adds 2 more only at high temperature; the question says moderate.","answer":"5 for diatomic and 3 for monatomic"},"phy-12-03-m07":{"idea":"The equipartition law says energy is shared equally among all the ways a molecule can hold it.","steps":["Each degree of freedom is a separate way to store energy.","At temperature T, every degree of freedom gets the same average energy.","That energy is ½kT per molecule.","Per mole, the same statement is ½RT."],"others":"(3/2)kT per molecule is the total for three translational degrees, not for one. kT per molecule is twice the right value. (1/2)RT is a per-mole value, so it cannot be 'per molecule'.","trap":"Do not mix ½kT (one molecule) with ½RT (one mole).","answer":"(1/2)kT per molecule"},"phy-12-03-m08":{"idea":"Mayer's relation connects the two molar heat capacities of an ideal gas.","steps":["At constant volume, all the heat goes into raising internal energy: Q = C_v ΔT.","At constant pressure, the gas also expands and does work PΔV = RΔT per mole.","So extra heat is needed: C_p ΔT = C_v ΔT + RΔT.","Hence C_p − C_v = R."],"others":"C_p/C_v = R mixes a ratio with a constant that has units of J/(mol K). C_p + C_v = R makes C_p smaller than R, which is impossible. C_p − C_v = R/2 has the wrong size for the work done.","trap":"C_p is always bigger than C_v, and the gap is exactly R.","answer":"C_p − C_v = R"},"phy-12-03-m09":{"idea":"γ is the ratio of the two heat capacities. It depends on the degrees of freedom f.","steps":["C_v = (f/2)R and C_p = (f/2 + 1)R.","So γ = C_p/C_v = 1 + 2/f.","Monatomic: f = 3, so γ = 1 + 2/3 = 5/3.","Rigid diatomic: f = 5, so γ = 1 + 2/5 = 7/5."],"others":"3/2 and 7/5 gives the wrong value for the monatomic gas. 4/3 and 5/3 is wrong for both gases. 7/5 and 5/3 has the correct values in the wrong order.","trap":"More degrees of freedom means a smaller γ.","answer":"5/3 and 7/5"},"phy-12-03-m10":{"idea":"An ideal gas has no forces between molecules, so it has no potential energy.","steps":["Internal energy is the sum of the molecular energies.","Without molecular forces, there is no potential energy stored.","So U is only the total kinetic energy of the molecules.","That is fixed by temperature: U = (f/2) nRT. Pressure and volume do not appear."],"others":"Volume does not matter because molecules do not attract each other. Pressure and volume together fail for the same reason, even if T changes the product. Pressure alone is also wrong, since it can change at constant T without changing U.","trap":"Constant temperature means constant U for an ideal gas, whatever P and V do.","answer":"its temperature"},"phy-12-03-m11":{"idea":"Boyle's law describes how a gas squeezes at constant temperature.","steps":["At constant T and fixed mass, PV = constant.","If the volume is halved, the pressure must double to keep the product.","So P is inversely proportional to V.","The graph of P against V is a curve that falls (a hyperbola)."],"others":"Direct proportion to volume would mean squeezing the gas lowers its pressure, which is false. Volume proportional to temperature is Charles's law, a different law. Pressure independent of volume contradicts PV = constant.","trap":"Do not confuse Boyle (constant T) with Charles (constant P).","answer":"the pressure is inversely proportional to the volume"},"phy-12-03-m12":{"idea":"Charles's law links volume and temperature at constant pressure, and temperature must be in kelvin.","steps":["At constant pressure and fixed mass, V/T = constant.","Here T is the absolute (kelvin) temperature.","So V is directly proportional to T.","If T doubles in kelvin, V doubles."],"others":"Proportional to °C is wrong because the volume is not zero at 0 °C. Pressure proportional to volume is not Charles's law. Inversely proportional to T would make a hotter gas shrink, which is false.","trap":"A Celsius temperature cannot be used in a ratio. Always convert to kelvin.","answer":"the volume is proportional to the absolute temperature"},"phy-12-03-m13":{"idea":"The kelvin and Celsius scales differ by a fixed shift of 273.15.","steps":["The relation is T = t + 273.15, with T in kelvin and t in degrees Celsius.","Put T = 0 K.","Then t = 0 − 273.15 = −273.15 °C.","This is the lowest possible temperature."],"others":"−373.15 °C subtracts 373.15, which is the boiling-point shift, not the zero point. −100 °C is only a round number with no meaning here. 0 °C is the freezing point of water, which is 273.15 K.","trap":"Do not confuse 273 (ice point) with 373 (steam point).","answer":"−273.15 °C"},"phy-12-03-m14":{"idea":"The mean free path is the average distance a molecule travels between two collisions.","steps":["The formula is λ = 1/(√2 π d² n).","Here d is the molecule diameter and n is the number of molecules per volume.","λ is inversely proportional to n.","Pumping gas out lowers n, so λ becomes larger."],"others":"It does not become zero, because λ is finite for any n above zero. It does not decrease: fewer neighbours mean fewer collisions. It does not stay the same, since n appears in the formula.","trap":"In a good vacuum, λ can become larger than the container.","answer":"increases"},"phy-12-03-m15":{"idea":"Avogadro's law says the number of molecules in a gas sample depends on P, V and T, not on the type of gas.","steps":["From PV = NkT, we get N = PV/(kT).","If P, V and T are the same for two gases, N is the same.","So equal volumes hold equal numbers of molecules.","The gas type does not appear anywhere."],"others":"Same speed is false: lighter molecules are faster at the same temperature. Same mass is false: a hydrogen molecule weighs much less than an oxygen molecule. Same number of atoms is false because molecules can have different numbers of atoms.","trap":"Molecules, not atoms. Hydrogen (H₂) has 2 atoms per molecule; helium has 1.","answer":"the same number of molecules"},"phy-12-03-m16":{"idea":"At constant volume, pressure is proportional to the absolute temperature (Gay-Lussac's law).","steps":["For a fixed mass in a rigid container, V is constant.","From PV = nRT, P/T = nR/V = constant.","So P is proportional to T.","T doubles, so P doubles."],"others":"Half as large would need T to be halved. Unchanged would mean P has no link to T, which is false. Four times as large squares the ratio, but P is linear in T.","trap":"The temperature must be in kelvin when taking the ratio.","answer":"twice as large"},"phy-12-03-m17":{"idea":"Temperature fixes the average kinetic energy, not the speed. Speed also depends on mass.","steps":["The average translational energy is (3/2)kT, so it depends only on T.","Two gases at the same T therefore have the same average kinetic energy.","Kinetic energy = ½mv², so a lighter molecule needs a higher speed for the same energy.","So the average speeds are different."],"others":"Different energies in proportion to mass is wrong; the energy is the same. The same speed with different energy is the reverse of the truth. Same speed and same energy cannot both hold when the masses differ.","trap":"Same T means same energy, not same speed.","answer":"the same average translational kinetic energy, but not the same average speed"},"phy-12-03-m18":{"idea":"Pressure is the average force per area from many molecular hits on the walls.","steps":["Each molecule hitting a wall bounces back and changes its momentum.","By Newton's second law, this change of momentum is a force on the wall.","Billions of hits each second add up to a steady force.","Force per unit area of this is the gas pressure."],"others":"Attraction of the walls is not part of the model. The weight of the gas is tiny and acts only downwards, but pressure acts equally in all directions. Repulsion between molecules is ignored in an ideal gas.","trap":"Pressure exists even in a small container in space, where weight plays no role.","answer":"the collisions of its molecules with the walls"},"phy-12-03-m19":{"idea":"Real gases follow PV = nRT best when molecular forces and molecule size do not matter.","steps":["At low pressure, the molecules are far apart, so the forces between them are tiny.","Molecule size is also small compared with the volume.","At high temperature, molecules move so fast that weak attractions cannot hold them.","So low pressure and high temperature give ideal behaviour."],"others":"High temperature with high pressure squeezes molecules close, so forces and size matter. Low temperature with low pressure lets the attractions act on slow molecules. Low temperature with high pressure is the worst case, close to liquefaction.","trap":"Remember: 'thin and hot' gas is ideal.","answer":"high temperature and low pressure"},"phy-12-03-m20":{"idea":"A black body is the ideal absorber of radiation.","steps":["When radiation hits a surface, part is reflected, part is transmitted and part is absorbed.","A black body absorbs all of it, at every wavelength.","So its absorptivity is 1.","It is also the best emitter at every temperature."],"others":"Reflecting all radiation describes a perfect mirror, the opposite of a black body. Absorbing only visible light is not a perfect absorber. Emitting only black colour makes no sense; a hot black body glows brightly.","trap":"A black body at high temperature is not black; the Sun is close to one.","answer":"absorbs all the radiation that falls on it"},"phy-12-03-m21":{"idea":"Stefan's law says the power radiated by a black body goes as the fourth power of its absolute temperature.","steps":["Stefan's law: E = σT⁴ per unit area.","The temperature becomes 2T.","New power = σ(2T)⁴ = 16 σT⁴.","So the power is 16 times as large."],"others":"4 times uses T². 2 times uses T¹. 8 times uses T³. The law has the power 4, and 2⁴ = 16.","trap":"Count the exponent: T to the power 4, so 2⁴ = 16.","answer":"16 times as large"},"phy-12-03-m22":{"idea":"Wien's law says that a hotter body shines most strongly at a shorter wavelength.","steps":["Wien's displacement law: λ_m T = constant.","If T goes up, λ_m must go down so the product stays the same.","So the peak moves to shorter wavelength.","This is why a heated iron goes from red to white."],"others":"A longer wavelength is the opposite direction. Zero at a finite temperature would need λ_m T to be zero, but the constant is not zero. Staying the same breaks the law.","trap":"Hotter means bluer (shorter wavelength).","answer":"becomes shorter"},"phy-12-03-m23":{"idea":"Kirchhoff's law connects how well a surface absorbs with how well it emits.","steps":["At a given T and wavelength, emissive power ÷ absorptive power is the same for all bodies.","This common value is the emissive power of a black body.","So a body with high absorptivity must also have high emissive power.","A good absorber is a good emitter."],"others":"A good absorber being a poor emitter is the reverse of the law. Reflectivity equal to emissivity is not stated by the law. Emission being independent of absorption is exactly what the law denies.","trap":"Good absorber, good emitter; poor absorber, poor emitter.","answer":"a good absorber is also a good emitter"},"phy-12-03-m24":{"idea":"Newton's law of cooling says a body cools faster when it is much hotter than its surroundings.","steps":["The rate of heat loss is proportional to (T − Tₛ).","Here T is the body temperature and Tₛ is the surroundings temperature.","This is written dT/dt = −k(T − Tₛ).","It holds only for a small temperature difference."],"others":"The fourth power of absolute temperature belongs to Stefan's law, not to Newton's. The mass of the surroundings does not enter the law. The body temperature alone ignores the surroundings, but a body in equilibrium with them does not cool.","trap":"The difference of temperature matters, not the temperature alone.","answer":"the difference between the temperature of the body and that of its surroundings"},"phy-12-03-m25":{"idea":"Conduction and convection need matter to carry heat. Radiation does not.","steps":["Conduction passes heat from atom to atom inside matter.","Convection moves heat by the flow of a fluid.","Radiation moves heat as electromagnetic waves.","These waves can cross empty space, as sunlight does."],"others":"Convection needs a flowing fluid. Conduction needs touching particles. 'Conduction and convection together' needs a medium twice over; neither works in vacuum.","trap":"The Sun warms the Earth across empty space only through radiation.","answer":"radiation"},"phy-12-03-m26":{"idea":"By Kirchhoff's law, a surface that absorbs well also emits well.","steps":["Dull black surfaces absorb most of the radiation that falls on them.","By Kirchhoff's law, they are therefore good emitters too.","Polished and white surfaces reflect most radiation.","So the dull black surface is best at both jobs."],"others":"Polished gold and polished silver reflect most radiation, so they absorb and emit poorly. A white painted surface also reflects a large part of visible light, so it is a poorer absorber.","trap":"This is why cooking pots are black on the bottom and kettles are shiny to keep heat in.","answer":"a dull black surface"},"phy-12-03-m27":{"idea":"The speeds of gas molecules are spread out, and different averages of that spread have different values.","steps":["The three speeds are v_mp = √(2kT/m), v_avg = √(8kT/πm) and v_rms = √(3kT/m).","Compare the numbers under the root: 2, 8/π ≈ 2.55 and 3.","3 is the biggest, so v_rms is the largest.","The order is v_mp < v_avg < v_rms."],"others":"Average speed has 2.55 under the root, smaller than 3. The most probable speed has 2, the smallest. 'All equal' is wrong because the three numbers differ.","trap":"Remember the order 2 < 2.55 < 3 for mp, avg, rms.","answer":"the root-mean-square speed"},"phy-12-03-n01":{"idea":"Use the ideal gas equation to find pressure when n, V and T are known.","steps":["PV = nRT, so P = nRT/V.","n = 2 mol, R = 8.314 J/(mol K), T = 300 K, V = 0.05 m³.","P = 2 × 8.314 × 300 / 0.05 = 4988.4 / 0.05 = 99768 Pa.","In kPa, P = 99.8 kPa."],"trap":"This is close to 1 atmosphere (101 kPa), which is sensible for room temperature.","answer":"99.8 kPa"},"phy-12-03-n02":{"idea":"The rms speed of molecules is found from temperature and molar mass.","steps":["v_rms = √(3RT/M), with M in kg/mol.","M = 32 g/mol = 0.032 kg/mol, T = 300 K.","3RT = 3 × 8.314 × 300 = 7482.6.","v_rms = √(7482.6 / 0.032) = √233831 ≈ 484 m/s."],"trap":"Convert M to kg/mol. Using 32 instead of 0.032 gives a wrong answer by a factor of about 30.","answer":"484 m/s"},"phy-12-03-n03":{"idea":"Average kinetic energy per molecule depends on temperature only.","steps":["KE = (3/2) k T.","k = 1.38 × 10⁻²³ J/K and T = 300 K.","KE = 1.5 × 1.38 × 10⁻²³ × 300 = 6.21 × 10⁻²¹ J.","In units of 10⁻²¹ J, this is 6.21."],"trap":"Use k (per molecule), not R.","answer":"6.21 × 10⁻²¹ J"},"phy-12-03-n04":{"idea":"Since v_rms depends on √T, doubling the speed needs four times the temperature.","steps":["v_rms is proportional to √T, so T is proportional to v².","The speed doubles: v₂ = 2v₁.","T₂/T₁ = (v₂/v₁)² = 2² = 4.","T₂ = 4 × 300 = 1200 K."],"trap":"Doubling the speed needs 4 times T, not 2 times.","answer":"1200 K"},"phy-12-03-n05":{"idea":"Kinetic theory gives pressure from density and rms speed.","steps":["P = ⅓ ρ v_rms².","ρ = 1.2 kg/m³ and v_rms = 500 m/s.","P = ⅓ × 1.2 × 500² = ⅓ × 1.2 × 250000 = 100000 Pa.","P = 100.0 kPa."],"trap":"Check units: kg/m³ × m²/s² = Pa.","answer":"100.0 kPa"},"phy-12-03-n06":{"idea":"The internal energy of an ideal gas is its total kinetic energy, f/2 per mole times RT.","steps":["A monatomic gas has f = 3.","U = (3/2) n R T.","n = 2 mol, R = 8.314 J/(mol K), T = 300 K.","U = 1.5 × 2 × 8.314 × 300 = 7482.6 J."],"answer":"7482.6 J"},"phy-12-03-n07":{"idea":"The heat capacity C_p of an ideal gas follows from its degrees of freedom and Mayer's relation.","steps":["A rigid diatomic gas has f = 5, so C_v = (5/2)R.","Mayer's relation: C_p = C_v + R.","C_p = (5/2)R + R = (7/2)R.","C_p = 3.5 × 8.314 = 29.10 J/(mol K)."],"trap":"Ratio check: C_p/C_v = 7/5 = 1.4, the known value for air.","answer":"29.10 J/(mol K)"},"phy-12-03-n08":{"idea":"Stefan's law gives the power radiated by a black body from its area and temperature.","steps":["P = σ A T⁴.","σ = 5.67 × 10⁻⁸ W/(m² K⁴), A = 0.5 m², T = 1000 K.","T⁴ = (10³)⁴ = 10¹² K⁴.","P = 5.67 × 10⁻⁸ × 0.5 × 10¹² = 28350 W."],"answer":"28350 W"},"phy-12-03-n09":{"idea":"Wien's law gives the wavelength where a hot body emits the most.","steps":["λ_m = b / T, where b is Wien's constant.","b = 2.898 × 10⁻³ m K and T = 5800 K.","λ_m = 2.898 × 10⁻³ / 5800 = 4.997 × 10⁻⁷ m.","1 nm = 10⁻⁹ m, so λ_m = 499.7 nm."],"trap":"499.7 nm lies in the visible green band, which fits sunlight.","answer":"499.7 nm"},"phy-12-03-n10":{"idea":"Radiated power goes as T⁴, so a ratio of temperatures is raised to the fourth power.","steps":["P is proportional to T⁴.","The factor is (T₂/T₁)⁴.","T₂/T₁ = 600/300 = 2.","Factor = 2⁴ = 16."],"trap":"Use kelvin for both temperatures.","answer":"16 times"}};
